    "description": {
      "type": "doc",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "text": "Chiang Mai sits in a valley on the Ping River, about 700 kilometres north of Bangkok. Doi Suthep rises to 1,676 metres on the western edge of the city, and higher ranges run on toward the Myanmar border.",
              "type": "text"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "text": "King Mangrai founded the city in 1296 as the capital of the Lanna kingdom. Brick walls and a square moat still frame the old city, which holds more than 30 temples, including Wat Chiang Man and Wat Chedi Luang. Saffron-robed monks walk the lanes at dawn. Khao soi, a curry noodle soup, and sai ua sausage are the northern dishes to order.",
              "type": "text"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "text": "November to February brings cool, dry days, and the Yi Peng lantern festival falls in November. March and April are hot and hazy from crop burning. Dress with covered shoulders and knees for temple visits.",
              "type": "text"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
